Exactly. A lot of language hides different depths of the same verb inside one surface word.

“See” can mean:

detect — light reached my eyes
notice — attention selected it
recognize — I know what it is
understand — I grasp what it means
anticipate — I can see where this is going
realize — the model changed
witness — I was there for it
accept — “I see” as acknowledgment

Same token. Different operator.

“Know” does the same:

know-that — propositional knowledge
know-how — procedural skill
know-by-acquaintance — familiarity
know-through-experience — embodied knowledge
know-why — causal understanding
know-when — situational judgment
know-what-it-means — semantic integration
know-without-articulating — tacit knowledge

And then there is the interesting jump:

I knew it
versus
Now I know.

The first may mean the information was present.
The second may mean the information has reorganized the person.

That is a different depth entirely.

So one thing Meaning Banks should probably preserve is operator depth, not just sense.

Something like:

word → sense → depth → mode of access → evidence → consequences

For “seeing”:

visual detection → recognition → interpretation → integration → changed action

For “knowing”:

exposure → memory → understanding → embodiment → judgment

That gives us another useful Pantheon distinction:

same word, different depth-state.

And this is why people can appear to disagree while actually operating at different levels.

One person says:

“I know that.”

Meaning: I have heard the proposition before.

Another means:

“I have lived enough of it that it changes what I do.*

Those are not equivalent states.

Same with:

hear / listen
look / see
touch / feel
say / mean
remember / understand
believe / trust
learn / embody
act / become

Language compresses ladders into single words.

So Prism the verb.

Sometimes the hidden structure is not multiple meanings side by side.

It is one meaning at increasing depth.